EFF's long word list: the notice of Massimo's copy

The list: the Electronic Frontier Foundation's list of 7,776 English
words for passphrases made with dice,

  https://www.eff.org/files/2016/07/18/eff_large_wordlist.txt
  (Last-Modified: Mon, 18 Jul 2016 22:41:51 GMT)

published with Joseph Bonneau's article of July 19, 2016,
https://www.eff.org/deeplinks/2016/07/new-wordlists-random-passphrases,
and linked from https://www.eff.org/dice. Neither page carries a licence
statement of its own; their footers link "Copyright (CC BY)" to
https://www.eff.org/copyright, read on 2026-11-25.

What that page states, in its words:

  "Any and all original material on the EFF website may be freely
  distributed at will under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
  International License (CC-BY), unless otherwise noted. All material
  that is not original to EFF may require permission from the copyright
  holder to redistribute."

Its words link the licence to https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/.
On the same page, beside those words, a badge (image alt "Creative
Commons License") is a link rel="license" to
http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/us/, the Creative Commons
licence of version 3.0 for the United States. Both are named here: the
words say CC BY 4.0 and the badge's link CC BY 3.0 US. The list is
EFF's own material with no other note, and Massimo takes the written
statement, CC BY 4.0, whose legal code is LICENSE beside this file.

The attribution EFF asks for, in its words:

  "If you redistribute something you got from the EFF site, it is
  appreciated if you make it known where the file originated, so people
  can get more info or updated versions."

